'''appendixious''' (ap-en-dik′shus), ''a.'' [< ''appendix'' + ''-i-ous.'' Cf. ML. ''appendicius'', supplementary.] Pertaining to or of the nature of an appendix; appendicatory. ''Bentham.'' [Rare.]

'''appense''' (a-pens′), ''a.'' [< L. ''appensus'', pp. of ''appendere'': see ''append''.] Hanging from above; specifically, in ''bot.'', pendulous: applied to ovules attached to the sides or angles of the ovary, and drooping. [Rare.]

'''appense†''' (a-pens′), ''v. t.'' [< OF. ''appenser'', append (a seal), < L. as if *''appensare'', freq. of ''appendere'', pp. ''appensus'', append: see ''append''.] To append (a seal).
{{smaller block|We haue caused . . . our seale thereunto to be ''appensed.''
{{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', II. 158. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}

readings.c2s4:
{{smaller block|(3) the act by which the mind is conscious immediately of the representative object, and through it mediately of the remote object represented. They called the first perception; the last, ''apperception''; the second, idea.
{{right|''Sir W. Hamilton'', Reid, p. 877, note.}}}}
Hence, by a slight modification —'''2.''' With Kant and most English writers, an act of voluntary consciousness, accompanied with self-consciousness: especially in the phrase ''pure apperception''.
{{smaller block|My theory, like Kant’s, lays ''apperception'', anglicè reflection, at the basis of philosophy.
{{right|''Hodgson'', Phil. of Reflection, I. 224.}}}}
'''3.''' In the psychology of Herbart (1776-1841), the coalescence of the remainder of a new isolated idea with an older one, by a modification of one or the other.—'''4.''' Apprehension; recognition.

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|The recognition or ''apperception'' of these truths by men.
{{right|''Maurice.'' (''N. E. D.'')}}}}

{{smaller|'''Active apperception.''' See ''active.''—'''Pure apperception,''' in the ''Kantian philos.'', the bare consciousness of self, the mere “I” or “I think.” See ''self-consciousness.''—'''Unity of apperception,''' that unity of consciousness by virtue of which its contents (perceptions, thoughts, etc.) coexist for it; the pure self or “I” to which the contents of one and the same mind must be referred.}}

'''apperceptive''' (ap-ėr-sep′tiv), ''a.'' [< ''apperception'', after ''perceptive.''] Pertaining or relating to, or of the nature of, apperception.
{{smaller block|It is after all nothing but our ''apperceptive'' faculties, potentially idealized, that are made to serve for the consciousness of a universal subject.
{{right|''Mind'', IX. 381.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Apperceptive union,''' the uniting of one idea with another by a voluntary act of consciousness.}}

'''apperil†''' (a-per′il), ''n.'' [< ''ap-''<sup>1</sup> + ''peril.''] Peril; danger; risk.
{{smaller block|Let me stay at thine ''apperil.''
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of A., i. 2.}}
Is there no law for a woman that will run upon a man at her own ''apperil?''
{{right|''Middleton'', Michaelmas Term, i. 1.}}}}

'''appersi-and†''' (ap′ėr-si-and′), ''n.'' Same as ''ampersand.''
{{smaller block|A shrivelled cadaverous piece of deformity in the shape of an izzard or an ''appersiand.''
{{right|''Macklin'', Man of the World, iii. 1.}}}}

'''appertain''' (ap-ėr-tān′), ''v. i.'' [Early mod. E. also ''appertane'', ''apertain'', < ME. ''apperteinen'', ''aperteinen'', ''apertenen'', < OF. ''apartenir'', F. ''appartenir'', < LL. ''appertinere'', belong to, < L. ''ad'', to, + ''pertinere'', belong, pertain: see ''pertain.''] To belong or pertain, as a part (to the whole), a member (to a class), a possession, or an attribute; belong by association or normal relation.

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>Hang mournful epitaphs, and do all rites
That ''appertain'' unto a burial.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, iv. 1.}}
<poem>The Father, to whom in heaven supreme
Kingdom, and power, and glory ''appertains.''</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., vi. 815.}}
In giving him to another, it [love] still more gives him to himself. . . . He does not longer ''appertain'' to his family and society; he is somewhat; he is a person.
{{right|''Emerson'', Essays, 1st ser., p. 161.}}
I am much inclined to suspect that the fossil upon which the genus Ornithopterus has been founded ''appertains'' to a true Bird.
{{right|''Huxley'', Anat. Vert., p. 232.}}}}
='''Syn.''' {{smaller|See ''pertain.''}}

'''appertainance''' (ap-ėr-tā′nạns), ''n.'' [< ''appertain'' + ''-ance.'' Cf. ''appertenance'' and ''appurtenance.''] '''1.''' The quality or state of appertaining. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The noblest elevations of the human mind have in ''appertainance'' their sands and swamps.
{{right|''Landor'', Imaginary Conversations. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''2.''' A thing which appertains; an appurtenance.

'''appertainment''' (ap-ėr-tān′mẹnt), ''n.'' [< ''appertain'' + ''-ment.''] That which appertains or belongs; an appurtenance; an external or adventitious attribute. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>We lay by
Our ''appertainments.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., ii. 3.}}}}

'''appertenance†''', '''appertenence†''', '''appertinence†''', ''n.'' Old forms of ''appurtenance.''

'''appertinent†''' (a-pėr′ti-nent), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Same as ''appurtenant'', after the L. ''appertinen(t-)s'': see ''appurtenant.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' Belonging; properly relating; appurtenant.
{{smaller block|All the other gifts ''appertinent'' to man.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., i. 2.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
'''II.''' ''n.'' That which appropriately belongs to something else; an appurtenance.
{{smaller block|<poem>You know how apt our love was, to accord
To furnish him with all ''appertinents''
Belonging to his honour.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., ii. 2.}}}}

'''appete†''', ''v. t.'' [ME. ''appeten'', < OF. ''appeter'', < L. ''appetere'', ''adpetere'', strive after, try to get, < ''ad'', to, + ''petere'', seek, aim at: see ''petition''.] To crave or long for; covet; desire. ''Chaucer.''

'''appetence, appetency''' (ap′ẹ̄-tens, -tẹn-si), ''n.'' [= F. ''appétence'', < L. ''appetentia'', < ''appeten(t-)s'', ''adpeten(t-)s'': see ''appetent''.] '''1.''' The act of seeking or craving after that which satisfies the affections, passions, or tastes; desire; inclination; propensity.
{{smaller block|I know not to what else we can better liken the strong ''appetence'' of the mind for improvement, than to a hunger and thirst after knowledge and truth.
{{right|''Everett'', Orations, II. 277.}}
They had a strong ''appetency'' for reading. {{right|''Merivale.''}}}}
Specifically—'''2.''' Strong natural craving for that which gratifies the senses; appetite; animal desire: as, “lustful ''appetence'',” ''Milton'', P. L., xi. 619.
{{smaller block|The innate aversion to any poison known to modern chemistry can, by persistent disregard, be turned into a morbid ''appetency'', vehement and persistent in proportion to the virulence of the poison.
{{right|''Pop. Sci. Mo.'', XXVIII. 518.}}}}
'''3.''' A mental tendency toward an end; a volition or desire.
{{smaller block|I shall occasionally employ the term ''appetency'' in the rigorous signification, as a genus comprehending under it both desires and volitions. {{right|''Sir W. Hamilton.''}}}}
